DESIGN — what we want to build

File: openOODA/DESIGN.oot
North star. SHIPPED.oot is what exists. SPRINT.oot is this week. PM.oot is the pin.

openOODA is a systems language for safe, fast software.
Humans and agents write code that is checked early.
Code runs only with a clear permission token.
Every function can carry its own tests.
The compiler gives short, fixable errors.
The product path today is a native compiler that emits C and runs through gcc.
The rest of this file is the want. Build toward it.

1. Language
  Short loop from idea to running code.
  requires and ensures bound what a function may accept and promise. Full proof later.
  Data layout: arrays of structs, zero-copy.
  Goal names: soa_layout (struct of arrays) and dod_layout (data-oriented layout).
  AST macros: rewrite the parse tree at compile time with normal functions.
  Type-state: prove lifecycles like Unopened to Opened at compile time.

2. Agent tools
  Machine-readable errors (--json-errors) plus ooda fix for E_CAP (missing token) hints.
  One-turn multi-fix later.
  ooda outline and ooda reflect. Measure token cut later.
  Fill a blank body with help at compile time.
  Idle compilers form a net that fuzzes while you sleep.

3. Safety
  Capability seals for files, exec, env, net, time, rand, alloc, threads, GPU.
  Finer tokens, object-caps, and OS isolation later.
  Time and entropy seals. Strong random and attested clock later.
  Heap quota. OS rlimit later.
  MAX_CYCLES on while, for, and recursion. Cgroup later.
  SECRET: sinks at compile time. Full field taint later.
  Contract fuzzer on int, bool, string, list. Mixed types later.
  ARC (free when the last ref drops). Pause-free GC later.
  Undo RAM for a few seconds.
  Hash valid call edges.
  Ghost-state: cancel a change before it commits.
  Mutate layout in RAM.

4. Compiler targets
  Floor: emit C, link the C runtime (chs_rt), then gcc.
  ooda run on native machine code.
  LLVM emit. Full self-host later.
  GPU and NPU backends, entered with a GPU token.
  WASM emit. Browser floor later.
  Bare metal, no extra runtime.
  Swap code in a running process.
  Cross-language link-time optimize. Import C at compile time.
  Bit-identical builds.
  RAM to a hash (Merkle) store.

5. Ecosystem
  Compiler and CLI rebuild themselves using the seed (the trusted first oodac binary).
  Package host, native language server, and registry later.
  Boundary rules, then a mint registry.
  Channels and actors.
  std split: core vs os. Grow the surface.
  Errors that say what to fix. Auto-apply later.
  Human pause (HITL). Interactive harness later.

6. Tension
  Fake layouts stay off by default. Turn them on with METAMORPHIC: path-a.
  Path-a is the small decoy path: fake pads so memory offsets change.
  Deterministic build is the default.
  ARC now. Undo later.
  Seal calls into C (FFI) with a token. Full OS dlopen later.

Language shape
  Files end in .oo
  let is immutable unless let mut
  No null. Option[T] and Result[T, E] with match
  Traits, not inheritance
  Refinement types and newtypes
  Must-use Result and Option
  Brace syntax. if, match, and blocks return values
  Keywords requires, ensures, spec sit on the function
  Grammar lives in ../ooda/ooda.ebnf
  Any .oo file is a module
  Aim for files under 256 lines

Moonshots
  A browser tab that cannot escape its token
  A package registry that cannot touch net or fs without a token from main
  A kernel in .oo that wires interrupts to tokens

These are wants.

